WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.080
Each neuron ends up with one kind of receptor.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.040
Does that mean it responds to only one odour?

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.900
The choice gives the neuron one kind of receptor.

4
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.500
That receptor's binding pocket sets which molecules it responds to.

5
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:20.553
But one pocket does not mean one odour.

6
00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:25.863
Del Mármol, Yedlin and Ruta solved structures of an insect receptor from the

7
00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:28.883
bristletail, called MhOR5.

8
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:34.917
It is built of four identical subunits, and it is itself an odorant-gated ion

9
00:00:34.917 --> 00:00:35.917
channel.

10
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.320
The plant compound eugenol and the repellent DEET sat in the same pocket.

11
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.460
They are chemically unrelated.

12
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.570
This insect channel is a contrast, not a template.

13
00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.590
Mammals use G-protein-coupled receptors.

14
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:56.813
Billesbølle and colleagues solved the active human receptor OR51E2, bound to

15
00:00:56.813 --> 00:00:57.813
propionate.

16
00:00:58.317 --> 00:01:02.277
It was a purified, engineered complex with a mini G protein.

17
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.930
Humans carry about four hundred odorant receptors of this type.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:08.950
Mice carry about a thousand.

19
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:16.393
In a cAMP assay, two pocket mutants gained responses to longer-chain fatty acids.

20
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:19.827
So is the mutant simply a better receptor?

21
00:01:20.787 --> 00:01:24.247
Take the mutant F155A, and give it propionate.

22
00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:28.350
The EC50 is the dose giving half the maximum.

23
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.710
At the wild-type EC50, what fraction does the mutant give?

24
00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:41.913
In this model, about 6 percent of its own maximum.

25
00:01:42.813 --> 00:01:45.413
Its EC50 is about 15 times higher.

26
00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:50.937
So the mutant is not simply better.

27
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:55.777
It lost potency for propionate and gained responses to longer chains.

28
00:01:56.697 --> 00:01:57.817
Its selectivity shifted.

29
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.760
The size of the shift is uncertain.

30
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.240
A second table in the paper gives a smaller one.

31
00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:07.903
These are functional values from cultured cells.

32
00:02:08.783 --> 00:02:10.103
They are not binding constants.

33
00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:14.407
And a flat response would not prove a ligand cannot bind.

34
00:02:15.347 --> 00:02:18.227
Coupling, trafficking or the assay could be failing instead.

35
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:20.997
Now look back at the three phases.

36
00:02:22.257 --> 00:02:23.257
Silence every gene.

37
00:02:23.917 --> 00:02:24.917
Let a few compete.

38
00:02:25.937 --> 00:02:26.937
Lock in the winner.

39
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.630
The lock holds one receptor in place.

40
00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:34.350
That receptor's pocket decides which molecules the neuron responds to.
